#!/usr/bin/env bash
# ^^^ for syntax checking in the editor only

##
## AgentsTools.MemberCommsGoogle.include -- the raw per-member Google comms
## operations across Drive, Sheets and Docs: identity, search, spreadsheet
## read/write/clear, document read/append, and comments on either file type.
##
## READ AND WRITE OPERATIONS SIT IN ONE FILE, so note which are which. The
## mutating ones are sheet-write, sheet-clear, doc-write and comment-post.
## Every one of them reports a failure as "whether anything changed is
## UNKNOWN", never as "nothing happened": a write that failed partway is
## indistinguishable from one that never started, and only one of those is safe
## to retry blindly.
##
## Sourced lazily from DistroAgentsTools.fn.sh; relies on being dot-sourced
## for $1/$@/$MDSC_CMD.
##
## EVERY OP HERE TAKES <team-member> FIRST, and that member IS the acting
## identity -- which is why `comms` is a namespace layer UNDER the member
## prefix, --member-comms-<service>-<verb>, rather than a prefix of its own.
## A Google refresh token belongs to exactly one account, so the member decides
## whose Drive is searched and whose Sheets are read.
##
## THE DISPATCHER REACHES THIS FILE BY THE --member-comms-google-* GLOB, and
## that route is only correct because every op of that name lives here. Adding
## a --member-comms-google-* op therefore costs no dispatcher line; moving one
## out to another include would silently break the glob's one precondition.
##
## NAMING: RESOURCE-FIRST, DELIBERATELY, AND HERE IS THE RULE.
## This family is --member-comms-google-<resource>-<verb>, one segment deeper
## than --member-comms-email-<verb>. The tree uses BOTH orders and the split is
## not arbitrary: a service with ONE primary resource names its ops verb-first
## (--member-comms-slack-delete-message, -edit-message, -search-messages,
## --member-comms-email-mark-seen), while secondary or co-equal resources group
## under a resource prefix (--member-comms-slack-file-fetch, -file-info,
## --intern-op-board-rename, --librarian-inbox-item-trash). Google has no
## primary resource -- sheet, file and comment are co-equal -- so resource-first
## is the correct form here and matches the slack-file-* precedent exactly.
## THIS IS WRITTEN DOWN BECAUSE IT LOOKS LIKE AN INCONSISTENCY AND IS NOT:
## --member-comms-google-sheet-read sits beside --magic-comms-trello-post-comment
## in the same tree, and without this note someone will eventually "fix" one of
## them in the wrong direction.
##
## SCOPE -- WHAT THIS FAMILY DELIBERATELY DOES NOT HAVE:
## There is NO --member-comms-google-check op, and its absence is a design
## decision rather than a gap. Slack, email and Trello each expose a cheap
## `-check` unread poll that the comms sweep calls every iteration. Google has
## no unread queue to poll; the nearest equivalents are change feeds, not an
## inbox, and a `-check` here would end up listing files on every heartbeat.
## magic-coordinator.communication-sweep.routine.md says so directly in its own
## Scope section: "Doesn't do: Google (Drive/Sheets) -- extended procedure, only
## when the task is actually searching/grooming, not a default **check** call."
## The real caller is named in that same file: magic-team.grooming.routine,
## which owns "deep classification/triage, Google Drive/Sheets, board-coverage
## diffing". Build against that caller, not against a sweep.
##
## THE API'S STATUS CODE IS NOT THE ANSWER TO THE QUESTION THE CALLER ASKED.
## An empty result must not read as success: a failed token exchange means the
## answer is UNKNOWN, not empty, and a failed API call means the rows are
## UNKNOWN, not zero. The worker distinguishes these by exit status (2 exchange
## failed, 3 call failed, 4 credential is dead and needs a human) and each arm
## below reports the outcome of the QUESTION its caller asked.
##
## DELIBERATE DIVERGENCE FROM THE OTHER COMMS INCLUDES -- READ BEFORE
## "FIXING" IT. The email, Slack and Trello includes repeat their member-
## validation-and-credential-resolution block inline in every arm. THIS FILE
## DOES NOT: it calls one AgentsToolsGoogleResolveMember helper instead. That
## is a decision, not drift, and it was reviewed and approved as one.
## The reason is arm count, not taste: those files carry two or three arms,
## this one carries four today and grows with the write side, and the repeated
## block is ~25 lines whose only per-arm variation is the op name in its error
## text. The helper takes that op name as an argument, so every message stays
## exactly as specific as the inline form; the ordering is unchanged; and all
## of it still runs before any network call. Nothing is centralised that was
## not already character-for-character identical. The inline pattern in the
## other three files reads as a consequence of their arm count rather than a
## principle those files defend -- but if that ever becomes wrong, change this
## deliberately too, rather than by symmetry with a neighbour. Full rationale
## sits above the helper's own definition below.
##
## CREDENTIALS REACH THE WORKER THROUGH THE ENVIRONMENT, NEVER argv AND NEVER
## A FILE. This is AgentsImapFetchMessage.py's already-established spelling,
## matched rather than reinvented -- see AgentsToolsGoogleCall below. One
## consequence is worth stating at the top: NO TEMP FILE EXISTS ANYWHERE IN
## THIS FAMILY, so there is no cleanup path to get wrong. That is the failure
## that once left netrc files holding a live SMTP password in $TMPDIR for ~14
## hours; a mechanism with nothing to clean up cannot repeat it.
##
## Supported operations:
##   --member-comms-google-whoami        --member-comms-google-file-find
##   --member-comms-google-sheet-read    --member-comms-google-sheet-info
##   --member-comms-google-sheet-write   --member-comms-google-sheet-clear
##   --member-comms-google-doc-read      --member-comms-google-doc-write
##   --member-comms-google-comment-read  --member-comms-google-comment-post
##
## STRUCTURAL OPERATIONS ARE OUT OF SCOPE, deliberately. spreadsheets.batchUpdate
## is a union of some forty structural request types (add/delete tab, merge,
## formatting, conditional rules, filters) and is NOT modelled here; neither is
## whole-body document replacement. If either is ever genuinely needed, one raw
## JSON passthrough operation is the right shape rather than a modelled surface
## that must track Google's own schema forever.
##

## Shared member-and-credential resolution for every arm in this file.
##
## DELIBERATE, REASONED DIVERGENCE FROM THE email/Trello INCLUDES, which repeat
## this block inline in each arm. Those files carry two or three arms; this one
## carries four and will carry more once the write side lands, and the repeated
## block is ~25 lines of validation whose ONLY per-arm variation is the op name
## in its error text. Taking the op name as an argument keeps every one of those
## messages exactly as specific as the inline form while removing the copies.
## Nothing is centralised that was not already identical, the ordering is
## unchanged, and all of it still runs BEFORE any network call so a bad name
## never reaches a server.
##
## Assigns, by design, into the CALLER's already-declared locals:
##   googleClientId  googleClientSecret  googleRefreshToken
AgentsToolsGoogleResolveMember() {
	local opName="$1"
	local memberName="$2"

	AgentsToolsAssertBareName "$memberName" "<team-member>" "$opName" || return 1
	case "$memberName" in
		routine-*)
			## A routine acting as caller has no skill directory and is not
			## expected to grow one -- same treatment, same reason, as
			## --member-comms-slack-read's own carve-out.
		;;
		*)
			if [ ! -d "$HOME/.claude/skills/$memberName" ] ; then
				echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --$opName: no such member skill directory: $HOME/.claude/skills/$memberName" >&2
				return 1
			fi
		;;
	esac

	## STRICTLY THE ACTING MEMBER'S OWN SCOPE -- no fallback to another
	## member's credentials, for the reason --member-comms-email-check states
	## in full: a fallback makes the <team-member> argument observably
	## meaningless for any member holding no keys of its own, which is exactly
	## the accepted-and-ignored defect that argument exists to end. It matters
	## more here than anywhere else in the package, because a Google refresh
	## token IS an identity: acting under the wrong one is not a degraded
	## result, it is impersonation that produces no error at all.
	##
	## NOTE the two kinds of value are NOT interchangeable. CLIENT_ID and
	## CLIENT_SECRET identify the APPLICATION and may legitimately match across
	## members; REFRESH_TOKEN identifies the USER and never may. Do not
	## "optimise" this into a shared lookup -- in this estate even the app
	## values differ per member, because each member's OAuth client lives in
	## its own organisation.
	googleClientId="$( DistroAgentsTools --agents-config-option "$memberName" --select GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID )"
	googleClientSecret="$( DistroAgentsTools --agents-config-option "$memberName" --select GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET )"
	googleRefreshToken="$( DistroAgentsTools --agents-config-option "$memberName" --select GOOGLE_REFRESH_TOKEN )"
	if [ -z "$googleClientId" ] || [ -z "$googleClientSecret" ] || [ -z "$googleRefreshToken" ] ; then
		echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --$opName: GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID/GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET/GOOGLE_REFRESH_TOKEN not fully set in ${memberName}'s own scope in .local/.agents" >&2
		return 1
	fi

	## An embedded newline means the stored config is corrupted, and it is
	## refused rather than stripped -- same refuse-don't-repair stance as the
	## Trello arms. The specific cost of repairing it silently is worse here:
	## a mangled refresh token comes back from Google as `invalid_grant`, which
	## this family reports as "the credential is dead, a human must perform the
	## consent again". That sends someone to redo an OAuth consent flow when
	## the real fault was a stray newline in a config file.
	local googleNewline=$'\n'
	case "$googleClientId$googleClientSecret$googleRefreshToken" in
		*"$googleNewline"*)
			echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --$opName: GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID/GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET/GOOGLE_REFRESH_TOKEN in ${memberName}'s own scope in .local/.agents contains a newline -- a Google credential never legitimately does, so the config is corrupted. The value is NOT stripped or repaired here: the resulting failure would surface as invalid_grant and be misread as a revoked credential needing a fresh human consent" >&2
			return 1
		;;
	esac
	return 0
}

## Exactly one content source, for the arms that take written content.
##
## The same rule --member-comms-email-send already enforces, and for the same
## reason: given two sources, any silent precedence order means the caller's
## other source was accepted and ignored, and they have no way to tell which
## one actually landed. Given none, an empty write is refused rather than
## quietly appending nothing and reporting success.
AgentsToolsGoogleAssertOneContentSource() {
	local opName="$1"
	local textArg="$2"
	local fromStdin="$3"
	local fromFile="$4"

	local sourceCount=0
	[ -n "$textArg" ] && sourceCount=$(( sourceCount + 1 ))
	[ -n "$fromStdin" ] && sourceCount=$(( sourceCount + 1 ))
	[ -n "$fromFile" ] && sourceCount=$(( sourceCount + 1 ))

	if [ "$sourceCount" -eq 0 ] ; then
		echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --$opName: a content source is required -- give trailing text, --from-stdin, or --from-file <path>. Nothing was written" >&2
		return 1
	fi
	if [ "$sourceCount" -gt 1 ] ; then
		echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --$opName: more than one content source given -- use exactly one of trailing text, --from-stdin, or --from-file <path>. Nothing was written, because any precedence chosen here would silently discard the others" >&2
		return 1
	fi
	if [ -n "$fromFile" ] && [ ! -f "$fromFile" ] ; then
		echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --$opName: no such file: $fromFile -- nothing was written" >&2
		return 1
	fi
	return 0
}

## One invocation of the Google worker, with the credentials passed ONLY through
## the environment.
##
## CREDENTIALS NEVER APPEAR ON A COMMAND LINE. argv is world-readable through
## `ps`, so a secret in an argument is published to every process on the box for
## the call's lifetime. The environment is the mechanism AgentsImapFetchMessage.py
## already established in this package for exactly this, and this is the same
## one rather than a second spelling of it. No temp file is involved either, so
## there is nothing to leak through an interrupt window and nothing to forget to
## remove -- the failure mode that once left netrc files holding a live SMTP
## password in $TMPDIR for ~14 hours.
AgentsToolsGoogleCall() {
	AGENTS_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID="$googleClientId" \
	AGENTS_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET="$googleClientSecret" \
	AGENTS_GOOGLE_REFRESH_TOKEN="$googleRefreshToken" \
	AGENTS_GOOGLE_OP="$1" \
	AGENTS_GOOGLE_QUERY="${googleQuery:-}" \
	AGENTS_GOOGLE_LIMIT="${googleLimit:-}" \
	AGENTS_GOOGLE_SHEET_ID="${googleSheetId:-}" \
	AGENTS_GOOGLE_RANGE="${googleRange:-}" \
	AGENTS_GOOGLE_RENDER="${googleRender:-}" \
	AGENTS_GOOGLE_FULLTEXT="${googleFullText:-}" \
	AGENTS_GOOGLE_INCLUDE_TRASHED="${googleIncludeTrashed:-}" \
	AGENTS_GOOGLE_RAW_QUERY="${googleRawQuery:-}" \
	AGENTS_GOOGLE_DOC_ID="${googleDocId:-}" \
	AGENTS_GOOGLE_FILE_ID="${googleFileId:-}" \
	AGENTS_GOOGLE_APPEND="${googleAppend:-}" \
	AGENTS_GOOGLE_USER_ENTERED="${googleUserEntered:-}" \
	python3 "$MDLT_ORIGIN/myx/myx.distro-agents/sh-lib/AgentsGoogleApiCall.py"
}

case "$1" in

		## WHICH ACCOUNT DOES THIS MEMBER'S CREDENTIAL ACTUALLY RESOLVE TO.
		## The Trello side already carries this question as
		## --member-comms-trello-whoami; this is the Google answer to it, in the
		## same KEY=VALUE consumable shape.
		##
		## THIS OP EXISTS BECAUSE THE FAILURE IT CATCHES IS OTHERWISE SILENT.
		## A refresh token minted by consenting as the wrong account leaves the
		## member acting as that other person on every single call, with correct
		## code, no error, and nothing in any output to notice it by. Run this
		## immediately after filing a token, and any time the acting identity
		## matters. It needs no scope beyond the Drive scope the family already
		## requires, so it costs nothing extra to grant.
		--member-comms-google-whoami)
			shift
			local memberName="$1"
			shift || true
			if [ -z "$memberName" ] ; then
				echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-whoami: syntax is <team-member> -- the acting member is required, since a refresh token resolves to exactly one account and this operation exists to say which one" >&2
				set +e ; return 1
			fi
			if [ $# -gt 0 ] ; then
				echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-whoami: takes exactly one argument" >&2
				set +e ; return 1
			fi
			local googleClientId googleClientSecret googleRefreshToken
			AgentsToolsGoogleResolveMember member-comms-google-whoami "$memberName" || { set +e ; return 1 ; }
			local googleRc=0
			AgentsToolsGoogleCall whoami || googleRc=$?
			if [ "$googleRc" -ne 0 ] ; then
				echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-whoami: COULD NOT DETERMINE which Google account ${memberName}'s credential resolves to (worker rc=$googleRc) -- the acting identity is UNKNOWN, which is NOT the same as anonymous; the worker's own diagnostic is above" >&2
				set +e ; return "$googleRc"
			fi
			return 0
		;;

		## Drive search -- the entry point for this family, since every other op
		## needs a file id and this is what produces one. Emits TSV:
		## id, name, mimeType, modifiedTime.
		##
		## TAKES A PLAIN SEARCH TERM AND WRAPS IT. Drive's `q` is a structured
		## query language, NOT a search box: a bare word like `ADR` is a syntax
		## error there rather than a match-anything, and forwarding one returns
		## HTTP 400. This op therefore builds the query around the term the
		## caller gave -- `name contains '<term>' and trashed=false` -- which is
		## what "find me this file" actually means. Confirmed the hard way: the
		## first two real invocations of this op, by a caller who reasonably
		## typed a bare name, both failed with `Invalid Value` on `q`.
		##
		## --full-text also matches document body content. Off by default
		## because it is markedly slower and returns hits from inside unrelated
		## files, which is not what someone searching by name expects.
		## --include-trashed keeps deleted files in the result; by default they
		## are excluded, since a trashed file is otherwise indistinguishable
		## from a live one and a caller may act on something already binned.
		## --raw-query forwards the argument verbatim as a full Drive query, for
		## structured searches such as
		## "mimeType='application/vnd.google-apps.spreadsheet' and trashed=false".
		## That was this op's original contract, so it stays reachable: making
		## the default safer must not quietly remove what already worked.
		--member-comms-google-file-find)
			shift
			local memberName="$1"
			shift || true
			if [ -z "$memberName" ] ; then
				echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-file-find: syntax is <team-member> <search-term> [--full-text] [--include-trashed] [--raw-query] [--limit <n>] -- the acting member is required, since a Drive search returns only what that identity can see" >&2
				set +e ; return 1
			fi
			local googleQuery="$1"
			shift || true
			if [ -z "$googleQuery" ] ; then
				echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-file-find: a search term is required -- an empty term is refused rather than silently listing the whole Drive" >&2
				set +e ; return 1
			fi
			local googleLimit="" googleFullText="" googleIncludeTrashed="" googleRawQuery=""
			while [ $# -gt 0 ] ; do
				case "$1" in
					--limit)
						shift
						if [ -z "$1" ] ; then
							echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-file-find: --limit requires a value" >&2
							set +e ; return 1
						fi
						googleLimit="$1" ; shift
					;;
					--full-text)
						googleFullText="true" ; shift
					;;
					--include-trashed)
						googleIncludeTrashed="true" ; shift
					;;
					--raw-query)
						googleRawQuery="true" ; shift
					;;
					*)
						echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-file-find: invalid option: $1" >&2
						set +e ; return 1
					;;
				esac
			done
			## Rejected rather than accepted-and-ignored: --raw-query means the
			## argument IS the whole query, so the flags that shape a wrapped
			## one have nothing to act on. Silently dropping them would let a
			## caller believe a filter applied when it never did.
			if [ -n "$googleRawQuery" ] && { [ -n "$googleFullText" ] || [ -n "$googleIncludeTrashed" ] ; } ; then
				echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-file-find: --raw-query cannot be combined with --full-text or --include-trashed -- with --raw-query the argument is the complete Drive query and those flags would have nothing to shape. Put the condition in the query itself instead" >&2
				set +e ; return 1
			fi
			local googleClientId googleClientSecret googleRefreshToken
			AgentsToolsGoogleResolveMember member-comms-google-file-find "$memberName" || { set +e ; return 1 ; }
			local googleRc=0
			AgentsToolsGoogleCall file-find || googleRc=$?
			if [ "$googleRc" -ne 0 ] ; then
				echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-file-find: COULD NOT SEARCH Drive as $memberName (worker rc=$googleRc) -- the result set is UNKNOWN, not empty; nothing is known from this call about whether any file matches. The worker's own diagnostic is above" >&2
				set +e ; return "$googleRc"
			fi
			return 0
		;;

		## Cell values for one A1 range, as TSV.
		##
		## TSV, NOT JSON, AT THE BOUNDARY -- the API speaks JSON `values`
		## arrays, but a range IS tabular and every other op in this tree is
		## shell-consumable. Making a caller parse JSON to read three cells
		## would make this the only op family in the package that demands it.
		##
		## TWO CONVERSION RULES THE CALLER CAN RELY ON, both stated because
		## leaving either implicit corrupts data silently:
		##  - ROWS ARE PADDED to the width of the requested range. Sheets omits
		##    trailing empty cells, so A1:D10 returns two fields for a row whose
		##    last two are blank; emitted short, every positional consumer
		##    (`awk -F'\t' '{print $4}'`) reads the wrong column with no error.
		##    Where the range does not fix a width (a bare tab name), the widest
		##    row returned is used instead.
		##  - TAB, NEWLINE, CARRIAGE RETURN AND BACKSLASH inside a cell are
		##    escaped as \t, \n, \r and \\ respectively. A cell may legitimately
		##    contain any of them, and emitted raw a tab becomes a new column
		##    and a newline becomes a new row. The escape is reversible: undo \\
		##    last.
		##
		## Values render as FORMATTED_VALUE by default -- what a human reading
		## the sheet sees. --unformatted returns the underlying value instead
		## (a date becomes its serial number). Neither is wrong, which is
		## exactly why the default is stated rather than inherited silently.
		--member-comms-google-sheet-read)
			shift
			local memberName="$1"
			shift || true
			if [ -z "$memberName" ] ; then
				echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-sheet-read: syntax is <team-member> <sheet-id> <a1-range> [--unformatted] -- the acting member is required, since a spreadsheet is readable only by identities it is shared with" >&2
				set +e ; return 1
			fi
			local googleSheetId="$1"
			shift || true
			local googleRange="$1"
			shift || true
			if [ -z "$googleSheetId" ] || [ -z "$googleRange" ] ; then
				echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-sheet-read: syntax is <team-member> <sheet-id> <a1-range> [--unformatted] -- both the spreadsheet id and an A1 range are required; a range is what fixes the column width the output is padded to" >&2
				set +e ; return 1
			fi
			local googleRender=""
			while [ $# -gt 0 ] ; do
				case "$1" in
					--unformatted)
						googleRender="UNFORMATTED_VALUE" ; shift
					;;
					*)
						echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-sheet-read: invalid option: $1" >&2
						set +e ; return 1
					;;
				esac
			done
			local googleClientId googleClientSecret googleRefreshToken
			AgentsToolsGoogleResolveMember member-comms-google-sheet-read "$memberName" || { set +e ; return 1 ; }
			local googleRc=0
			AgentsToolsGoogleCall sheet-read || googleRc=$?
			if [ "$googleRc" -ne 0 ] ; then
				echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-sheet-read: COULD NOT READ $googleRange as $memberName (worker rc=$googleRc) -- the cell values are UNKNOWN, not empty. A range that is genuinely empty succeeds and prints no rows, which is a DIFFERENT outcome from this one. The worker's own diagnostic is above" >&2
				set +e ; return "$googleRc"
			fi
			return 0
		;;

		## Tab names and grid dimensions for one spreadsheet -- what a caller
		## needs before it can build a range for --member-comms-google-sheet-read.
		## Prints SPREADSHEET_TITLE=<title> first, then a TSV table of tabs with
		## its own header row: TAB_TITLE, TAB_ID, TAB_INDEX, ROWS, COLUMNS.
		--member-comms-google-sheet-info)
			shift
			local memberName="$1"
			shift || true
			if [ -z "$memberName" ] ; then
				echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-sheet-info: syntax is <team-member> <sheet-id> -- the acting member is required, since a spreadsheet is readable only by identities it is shared with" >&2
				set +e ; return 1
			fi
			local googleSheetId="$1"
			shift || true
			if [ -z "$googleSheetId" ] ; then
				echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-sheet-info: a spreadsheet id is required" >&2
				set +e ; return 1
			fi
			if [ $# -gt 0 ] ; then
				echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-sheet-info: invalid option: $1" >&2
				set +e ; return 1
			fi
			local googleClientId googleClientSecret googleRefreshToken
			AgentsToolsGoogleResolveMember member-comms-google-sheet-info "$memberName" || { set +e ; return 1 ; }
			local googleRc=0
			AgentsToolsGoogleCall sheet-info || googleRc=$?
			if [ "$googleRc" -ne 0 ] ; then
				echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-sheet-info: COULD NOT READ spreadsheet metadata as $memberName (worker rc=$googleRc) -- the tab list is UNKNOWN, not empty; the worker's own diagnostic is above" >&2
				set +e ; return "$googleRc"
			fi
			return 0
		;;

		## Write TSV into one A1 range. Content arrives on stdin or from a
		## file -- never as trailing text arguments, because a spreadsheet
		## range is tabular and a shell word is not.
		##
		## THE INPUT IS THE EXACT FORMAT --member-comms-google-sheet-read
		## EMITS, so a range can be read, edited in a pipeline, and written
		## straight back. Tab, newline, carriage return and backslash inside a
		## cell are written as \t, \n, \r and \\ and are un-escaped here; the
		## round trip is byte-exact.
		##
		## VALUES ARE STORED RAW BY DEFAULT, AND THAT IS A SAFETY DECISION.
		## Under --user-entered Google parses each value as though typed by a
		## person, so any cell beginning `=` becomes a live formula in a
		## document real people open. RAW stores exactly what was given.
		--member-comms-google-sheet-write)
			shift
			local memberName="$1"
			shift || true
			local googleSheetId="$1"
			shift || true
			local googleRange="$1"
			shift || true
			if [ -z "$memberName" ] || [ -z "$googleSheetId" ] || [ -z "$googleRange" ] ; then
				echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-sheet-write: syntax is <team-member> <sheet-id> <a1-range> [--append] [--user-entered] (--from-stdin|--from-file <path>) -- the acting member, the spreadsheet and the range are all required" >&2
				set +e ; return 1
			fi
			local googleAppend="" googleUserEntered="" googleFromFile="" googleFromStdin=""
			while [ $# -gt 0 ] ; do
				case "$1" in
					--append)			googleAppend="true" ; shift ;;
					--user-entered)		googleUserEntered="true" ; shift ;;
					--from-stdin)		googleFromStdin="true" ; shift ;;
					--from-file)
						shift
						if [ -z "$1" ] ; then
							echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-sheet-write: --from-file requires a path" >&2
							set +e ; return 1
						fi
						googleFromFile="$1" ; shift
					;;
					*)
						echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-sheet-write: invalid option: $1" >&2
						set +e ; return 1
					;;
				esac
			done
			## Exactly one content source, the same rule --member-comms-email-send
			## enforces: more than one is an error rather than a silent priority.
			if [ -n "$googleFromFile" ] && [ -n "$googleFromStdin" ] ; then
				echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-sheet-write: --from-stdin and --from-file given together -- use one or the other" >&2
				set +e ; return 1
			fi
			if [ -z "$googleFromFile" ] && [ -z "$googleFromStdin" ] ; then
				echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-sheet-write: a content source is required -- give --from-stdin or --from-file <path>. TSV rows only; trailing text arguments are not accepted, since a range is tabular" >&2
				set +e ; return 1
			fi
			if [ -n "$googleFromFile" ] && [ ! -f "$googleFromFile" ] ; then
				echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-sheet-write: no such file: $googleFromFile -- nothing was written" >&2
				set +e ; return 1
			fi
			local googleClientId googleClientSecret googleRefreshToken
			AgentsToolsGoogleResolveMember member-comms-google-sheet-write "$memberName" || { set +e ; return 1 ; }
			local googleRc=0
			if [ -n "$googleFromFile" ] ; then
				AgentsToolsGoogleCall sheet-write < "$googleFromFile" || googleRc=$?
			else
				AgentsToolsGoogleCall sheet-write || googleRc=$?
			fi
			if [ "$googleRc" -ne 0 ] ; then
				echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-sheet-write: COULD NOT WRITE $googleRange as $memberName (worker rc=$googleRc) -- whether anything was changed is UNKNOWN, and it must NOT be assumed nothing was; the worker's own diagnostic is above" >&2
				set +e ; return "$googleRc"
			fi
			return 0
		;;

		## Clear the values in one A1 range.
		##
		## ITS OWN OPERATION RATHER THAN A FLAG ON sheet-write, deliberately:
		## it destroys data, it takes no content, and this package names
		## destructive operations separately for exactly that reason --
		## --member-comms-slack-delete-message, --intern-op-board-trash,
		## --librarian-inbox-item-trash. A destructive mode hidden behind a
		## flag on a constructive verb reads as safer at the call site than it
		## is.
		--member-comms-google-sheet-clear)
			shift
			local memberName="$1"
			shift || true
			local googleSheetId="$1"
			shift || true
			local googleRange="$1"
			shift || true
			if [ -z "$memberName" ] || [ -z "$googleSheetId" ] || [ -z "$googleRange" ] ; then
				echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-sheet-clear: syntax is <team-member> <sheet-id> <a1-range> -- the acting member, the spreadsheet and the range are all required. The range is required and is never defaulted: there is no whole-sheet shorthand here, because a mistyped default would erase a spreadsheet" >&2
				set +e ; return 1
			fi
			if [ $# -gt 0 ] ; then
				echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-sheet-clear: invalid option: $1" >&2
				set +e ; return 1
			fi
			local googleClientId googleClientSecret googleRefreshToken
			AgentsToolsGoogleResolveMember member-comms-google-sheet-clear "$memberName" || { set +e ; return 1 ; }
			local googleRc=0
			AgentsToolsGoogleCall sheet-clear || googleRc=$?
			if [ "$googleRc" -ne 0 ] ; then
				echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-sheet-clear: COULD NOT CLEAR $googleRange as $memberName (worker rc=$googleRc) -- whether anything was cleared is UNKNOWN; the worker's own diagnostic is above" >&2
				set +e ; return "$googleRc"
			fi
			return 0
		;;

		## Plain text of one Google Doc.
		##
		## Paragraph text runs only: tables, embedded objects and footnotes are
		## NOT rendered. Stated here and in the help rather than left to be
		## inferred, because output that silently omits a table looks complete.
		--member-comms-google-doc-read)
			shift
			local memberName="$1"
			shift || true
			local googleDocId="$1"
			shift || true
			if [ -z "$memberName" ] || [ -z "$googleDocId" ] ; then
				echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-doc-read: syntax is <team-member> <doc-id> -- the acting member is required, since a document is readable only by identities it is shared with" >&2
				set +e ; return 1
			fi
			if [ $# -gt 0 ] ; then
				echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-doc-read: invalid option: $1" >&2
				set +e ; return 1
			fi
			local googleClientId googleClientSecret googleRefreshToken
			AgentsToolsGoogleResolveMember member-comms-google-doc-read "$memberName" || { set +e ; return 1 ; }
			local googleRc=0
			AgentsToolsGoogleCall doc-read || googleRc=$?
			if [ "$googleRc" -ne 0 ] ; then
				echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-doc-read: COULD NOT READ document $googleDocId as $memberName (worker rc=$googleRc) -- the content is UNKNOWN, not empty; the worker's own diagnostic is above" >&2
				set +e ; return "$googleRc"
			fi
			return 0
		;;

		## Append text to the end of one Google Doc.
		##
		## APPEND ONLY, DELIBERATELY. Replacing a document's whole body means
		## computing and deleting its existing content range first, which is
		## destructive and structural. If that is ever wanted it belongs in its
		## own operation, on the same reasoning that gave sheet-clear its own
		## name instead of a flag.
		--member-comms-google-doc-write)
			shift
			local memberName="$1"
			shift || true
			local googleDocId="$1"
			shift || true
			if [ -z "$memberName" ] || [ -z "$googleDocId" ] ; then
				echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-doc-write: syntax is <team-member> <doc-id> (<text...>|--from-stdin|--from-file <path>) -- the acting member and the document are both required" >&2
				set +e ; return 1
			fi
			local googleFromFile="" googleFromStdin="" googleText=""
			while [ $# -gt 0 ] ; do
				case "$1" in
					--from-stdin)		googleFromStdin="true" ; shift ;;
					--from-file)
						shift
						if [ -z "$1" ] ; then
							echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-doc-write: --from-file requires a path" >&2
							set +e ; return 1
						fi
						googleFromFile="$1" ; shift
					;;
					*)
						if [ -n "$googleText" ] ; then
							googleText="$googleText $1"
						else
							googleText="$1"
						fi
						shift
					;;
				esac
			done
			AgentsToolsGoogleAssertOneContentSource member-comms-google-doc-write "$googleText" "$googleFromStdin" "$googleFromFile" || { set +e ; return 1 ; }
			local googleClientId googleClientSecret googleRefreshToken
			AgentsToolsGoogleResolveMember member-comms-google-doc-write "$memberName" || { set +e ; return 1 ; }
			local googleRc=0
			if [ -n "$googleFromFile" ] ; then
				AgentsToolsGoogleCall doc-write < "$googleFromFile" || googleRc=$?
			elif [ -n "$googleFromStdin" ] ; then
				AgentsToolsGoogleCall doc-write || googleRc=$?
			else
				printf '%s\n' "$googleText" | AgentsToolsGoogleCall doc-write || googleRc=$?
			fi
			if [ "$googleRc" -ne 0 ] ; then
				echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-doc-write: COULD NOT APPEND to document $googleDocId as $memberName (worker rc=$googleRc) -- whether anything was written is UNKNOWN, and it must NOT be assumed nothing was; the worker's own diagnostic is above" >&2
				set +e ; return "$googleRc"
			fi
			return 0
		;;

		## Comments on one Drive file -- works for a Doc and a Sheet alike,
		## since comments are a Drive resource rather than a per-type one.
		## Emits TSV with its own header row.
		--member-comms-google-comment-read)
			shift
			local memberName="$1"
			shift || true
			local googleFileId="$1"
			shift || true
			if [ -z "$memberName" ] || [ -z "$googleFileId" ] ; then
				echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-comment-read: syntax is <team-member> <file-id> -- the acting member is required, since comments are visible only to identities the file is shared with" >&2
				set +e ; return 1
			fi
			if [ $# -gt 0 ] ; then
				echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-comment-read: invalid option: $1" >&2
				set +e ; return 1
			fi
			local googleClientId googleClientSecret googleRefreshToken
			AgentsToolsGoogleResolveMember member-comms-google-comment-read "$memberName" || { set +e ; return 1 ; }
			local googleRc=0
			AgentsToolsGoogleCall comment-read || googleRc=$?
			if [ "$googleRc" -ne 0 ] ; then
				echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-comment-read: COULD NOT READ comments on $googleFileId as $memberName (worker rc=$googleRc) -- the comment list is UNKNOWN, not empty; the worker's own diagnostic is above" >&2
				set +e ; return "$googleRc"
			fi
			return 0
		;;

		## Post one comment onto one Drive file, authored by <team-member>'s own
		## identity. Prints COMMENT_ID= and COMMENT_CREATED= on success, and
		## treats a response carrying no id as UNKNOWN rather than success.
		--member-comms-google-comment-post)
			shift
			local memberName="$1"
			shift || true
			local googleFileId="$1"
			shift || true
			if [ -z "$memberName" ] || [ -z "$googleFileId" ] ; then
				echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-comment-post: syntax is <team-member> <file-id> (<text...>|--from-stdin|--from-file <path>) -- the acting member is required, since a comment is authored by one identity and that member decides which account signs it" >&2
				set +e ; return 1
			fi
			local googleFromFile="" googleFromStdin="" googleText=""
			while [ $# -gt 0 ] ; do
				case "$1" in
					--from-stdin)		googleFromStdin="true" ; shift ;;
					--from-file)
						shift
						if [ -z "$1" ] ; then
							echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-comment-post: --from-file requires a path" >&2
							set +e ; return 1
						fi
						googleFromFile="$1" ; shift
					;;
					*)
						if [ -n "$googleText" ] ; then
							googleText="$googleText $1"
						else
							googleText="$1"
						fi
						shift
					;;
				esac
			done
			AgentsToolsGoogleAssertOneContentSource member-comms-google-comment-post "$googleText" "$googleFromStdin" "$googleFromFile" || { set +e ; return 1 ; }
			local googleClientId googleClientSecret googleRefreshToken
			AgentsToolsGoogleResolveMember member-comms-google-comment-post "$memberName" || { set +e ; return 1 ; }
			local googleRc=0
			if [ -n "$googleFromFile" ] ; then
				AgentsToolsGoogleCall comment-post < "$googleFromFile" || googleRc=$?
			elif [ -n "$googleFromStdin" ] ; then
				AgentsToolsGoogleCall comment-post || googleRc=$?
			else
				printf '%s\n' "$googleText" | AgentsToolsGoogleCall comment-post || googleRc=$?
			fi
			if [ "$googleRc" -ne 0 ] ; then
				echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --member-comms-google-comment-post: COULD NOT POST a comment on $googleFileId as $memberName (worker rc=$googleRc) -- whether the comment was posted is UNKNOWN; the worker's own diagnostic is above" >&2
				set +e ; return "$googleRc"
			fi
			return 0
		;;

		*)
			echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD: unknown --member-comms-google-* operation: $1 -- the supported operations are --member-comms-google-whoami, --member-comms-google-file-find, --member-comms-google-sheet-read, --member-comms-google-sheet-info, --member-comms-google-sheet-write, --member-comms-google-sheet-clear, --member-comms-google-doc-read, --member-comms-google-doc-write, --member-comms-google-comment-read and --member-comms-google-comment-post" >&2
			set +e ; return 1
		;;

esac
